   "id": "b2-20",
   "name": "Preventing and eliminating plant pests and diseases"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Pests are animals that damage crops: insects (aphids, locusts, caterpillars, weevils in stored grain), rodents (rats), birds, nematodes.\nPlant diseases are caused by germs: fungi (cocoa black pod), viruses (cassava mosaic, spread by whiteflies; maize streak, spread by leafhoppers) and bacteria.\nSigns: holes in leaves, wilting, yellow patterns (mosaic), rotting pods, poor yields.\nPrevention:\n• plant resistant varieties and clean, healthy seeds or cuttings;\n• crop rotation (do not plant the same crop on the same land every year);\n• weeding and good spacing; good drainage;\n• removing and burning infected plants (roguing), and harvesting black pods quickly;\n• drying and storing grain well.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "c": "cassava"
  },
  "problem": "A farmer's {c} leaves show yellow and green patches and are twisted. What is the disease, and how can he stop it spreading?",
  "steps": [
   "Yellow-green patches and twisted leaves are signs of cassava mosaic, a virus spread by whiteflies.",
   "He should pull up and burn the infected plants and plant clean cuttings of a resistant variety next time."
  ],
  "answer": "Cassava mosaic; remove infected plants and use clean, resistant cuttings.",
  "checks": []
 },
